# Windows Build Environment (MSYS2 UCRT64)

Humans build from an MSYS2 UCRT64 terminal, where `g++`, `cmake`, and `make` are already on `PATH`. The README and `DEVELOPMENT.md` document those direct commands.

Agents on Windows run from PowerShell and do not inherit that shell. Enter it with `scripts/Invoke-Ucrt64.ps1`. The script finds a normal MSYS2 install (`MSYS2_ROOT`, or the usual locations) and runs the command inside UCRT64. Set `MSYS2_ROOT` when the install is somewhere else.

Carcer uses CMake presets with Ninja. After `scripts/setup-dev.sh`, the normal developer entry point is the convenience Makefile under `src/`:

- Windows agents: `.\scripts\Invoke-Ucrt64.ps1 "make -C src"`
- Linux/macOS, and a human UCRT64 terminal: `make -C src`

The Makefile delegates to CMake; use explicit presets for qualification work.

From PowerShell, run UCRT64 compiler and bash commands through the wrapper:

```powershell
.\scripts\Invoke-Ucrt64.ps1 "cmake --preset ucrt64-debug"
.\scripts\Invoke-Ucrt64.ps1 "cmake --build --preset ucrt64-debug --target CARCER"
.\scripts\Invoke-Ucrt64.ps1 "./scripts/run-all-tests-ucrt64.sh"
.\scripts\Invoke-Ucrt64.ps1 "./scripts/update-translations.sh"
```

Explicit UCRT64 qualification uses `build/cmake/ucrt64-debug/compile_commands.json`.

## clangd (any MSYS2 UCRT64 install)

`setup-dev.sh` runs `scripts/generate-clangd-config.sh`. Agents should run that
script after a fresh configure, after the toolchain moves, or when clangd
diagnostics or go-to-definition are wrong:

```powershell
.\scripts\Invoke-Ucrt64.ps1 "./scripts/generate-clangd-config.sh"
```

The script reads the live UCRT64 prefix (`MINGW_PREFIX`, `c++ -dumpversion`,
`clang++ -print-resource-dir`) and writes:

- gitignored `.clangd` (compile database plus that install's libstdc++ and Clang resource dir)
- `clangd.path` in `.vscode/settings.json`, set to that install's `ucrt64/bin/clangd.exe`

Leave `.clangd` to the generator. `clangd.path` must be that `clangd.exe`.
A `.cmd` file or other wrapper breaks the language-server pipe, so Alt-click
never resolves. The UCRT64 environment needs `mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-clang` and
`mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-clang-tools-extra`. On Linux and macOS, clear
`clangd.path` and use `clangd` from `PATH`.
